    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Baekje founded its capital in the area in 18 BCE, Silla took the region in 553, and Goryeo built a southern capital there before the Joseon dynasty made Hanyang its planned capital in 1394.</li><li>Under Japanese rule from 1910 the city was renamed Gyeongseong and lost its status as a capital; it took the name Seoul after 1945, and four battles for it in the Korean War left it mostly in ruins.</li><li>After the war the city was rebuilt and in 1963 more than doubled in size, taking in Gangnam; its population peaked at over 10 million and has since decreased.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> Seoul changed hands four times between June 1950 and the spring of 1951, and one estimate puts the damage at no fewer than 191,000 buildings, 55,000 houses and 1,000 factories in ruins.</p></aside>
    </section>

